What this fork changes
----------------------

MAX Engine starts from Morphe Patcher 2.3.0 and, at 0.1.0, changes nothing about patching itself.
The differences are all about how the engine is built and distributed:

- The Gradle project is named max-engine and its version is its own, independent of MAX Patcher's
  and of MAX Patches'.
- Its libraries (the ARSCLib fork and the smali fork) are built from vendored sources pinned to
  exact commits instead of being resolved from JitPack or the GitHub Packages registry.
- It is consumed as a Gradle included build by MAX Patcher, so the app no longer downloads the
  patcher from an authenticated package registry.
- The public API is unchanged. `api/morphe-patcher.api`, the binary-compatibility dump, is carried
  over untouched: any change to the ABI shows up as a diff in that file, and CI fails on it.

Java package names (`app.morphe.patcher.*`) are deliberately unchanged. Every patch bundle and every
patch source in circulation is compiled against those names; renaming them would break patch
compatibility for no user-visible gain. GPLv3 Section 7c asks for the product to be marked as
different, which the project name, version, NOTICE and repository do.

